# Agents at scale

A "swarm" is one move repeated: split a task into many pieces, run an agent on each piece
*at the same time*, then synthesize the results. The intelligence is in the pieces; the
hard engineering is usually the orchestration — a job queue, a worker pool, a result bus,
backpressure, retries. On the [Nexus](/introduction/what-is-the-nexus) that machinery is
the BEAM's day job, so the whole orchestration is a few lines in a `server` block, and
"five agents" and "five hundred agents" are the same code.

This lesson builds a research swarm — the distilled core of our own Search Swarm — end
to end: plan, fan out, ground each agent in real pages, synthesize, and account the cost.

## The shape

Three steps: one LLM call decomposes the task into sub-questions, one agent runs per
sub-question *concurrently*, and one call folds the findings into an answer.

```
server :swarm do
  def run(task) do
    task
    |> plan()                                  # 1 LLM call → N focused sub-questions
    |> Enum.map(fn q -> Task.async(fn -> worker(q) end) end)
    |> Task.await_many(:infinity)              # every agent runs in parallel
    |> synthesize(task)                        # fold all findings into one answer
  end
end
```

`Task.async` over a list and `Task.await_many` *is* the concurrency model — there is no
queue to configure and no worker count to tune. The list's length is the swarm's size.

## The worker: grounded, not guessed

Each agent searches, pulls real pages with the built-in browser, and answers **only**
from what it actually read — so the findings are grounded, not hallucinated:

```
  defp plan(task) do
    {:ok, %{content: c}} =
      Nexus.Llm.complete([%{role: "user", content:
        "Break this into 8 distinct, focused sub-questions, one per line:\n#{task}"}], [])

    c |> String.split("\n") |> Enum.map(&String.trim/1) |> Enum.reject(&(&1 == ""))
  end

  defp worker(question) do
    # one web-grounded search returns candidate source URLs…
    {:ok, %{annotations: cites}} =
      Nexus.Llm.complete([%{role: "user", content: question}], model: "…:online")

    # …pull the top pages with the nexus browser and answer from their text
    pages =
      cites
      |> Enum.take(3)
      |> Enum.map(fn c -> Nexus.Browse.read(c["url"]) end)
      |> Enum.flat_map(fn {:ok, p} -> [p.text]; _ -> [] end)

    {:ok, %{content: finding}} =
      Nexus.Llm.complete([%{role: "user", content:
        "Answer from these pages only, and cite them:\n#{question}\n\n#{Enum.join(pages, "\n\n")}"}], [])

    %{question: question, finding: finding}
  end
```

`Nexus.Llm.complete` (with `:online` for web search) and `Nexus.Browse.read` (the
runtime's headless browser) are host primitives — the worker just calls them. The
`annotations` are the real source URLs the model cited; the agent then reads those pages
itself before writing a word.

## Synthesize — and pay the real bill

The last step merges the findings, and because every model call goes through one place,
you can ask the provider for the **actual** cost and stream it out — what's shown is
credits charged, not an estimate:

```
  defp synthesize(findings, task) do
    notes = Enum.map_join(findings, "\n\n", fn f -> "## #{f.question}\n#{f.finding}" end)

    {:ok, %{content: report, usage: u}} =
      Nexus.Llm.complete([%{role: "user", content:
        "Write one cited report answering: #{task}\n\nFindings:\n#{notes}"}], usage: %{include: true})

    %{report: report, cost_usd: u["cost"]}
  end
end
```

## Why it stays small

- **No queue, no workers, no bus.** `Task.async` + the BEAM scheduler *is* the
  infrastructure — the runtime runs thousands of lightweight processes.
- **Real tools, brokered.** `Nexus.Llm.complete` and `Nexus.Browse.read` are host
  primitives; SSRF-gated and cost-accounted. → [Sandboxing & the Dock](/capabilities/sandboxing-and-the-dock)
- **One `server` unit** holds the whole orchestration. To stream it live to a browser as
  it runs, register it as a [live source](/serving/routes-and-pages); to persist each run,
  write a [`resource`](/data/resources-and-the-store).

Swap the `worker` for whatever the job is — a [coding agent](/examples/coding-agent), an
[experiment](/examples/autonomous-research) — and the same fan-out runs it at scale.
